europe's digital economy data privacy  and suspected electronic eavesdropping  by u.s intelligence services were  highlights as eu leaders met in brussels  the leaders pledged to bring down  digital borders visible for example  through online shopping or mobile phone  roaming charges and to encourage  investment in high-speed broadband and  cloud computing  they also vowed to improve trust between  european citizens and online providers  and to ensure that people have better  control over their personal data  on transatlantic data privacy france and  germany planned to seek talks with the  united states on intelligence gathering  methods and they welcomed other eu  partners to join them  heads of state and government underlined  the close relationship  between europe and the united states  and the value of that partnership  they expressed their conviction that the  partnership must be based on respect and  on trust  including as concerns the work and  cooperation  of secret services  on economic matters the leaders  discussed ways to strengthen the banking  union and keep up momentum on the eu's  roadmap for economic and monetary union  as well as to bolster growth and  confidence in the euro  they expressed determination to tackle  youth unemployment and improve the  availability of finances for small and  medium-sized businesses  preventing tragedies in the  mediterranean was also high on the  agenda the leaders agreed that  determined action must be taken  they want to address the root causes by  working with countries that asylum  seekers leave or transit leaders also  want to step up the fight against human  trafficking they said decisions on  better protecting would-be immigrants  would be taken at their next meeting in  december finally they're hopeful that  they can take major steps forward at the  eastern partnership summit in vilnius  next month they urged ukraine to make  more progress notably on selective  justice
 

